{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/7/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\" lines=\"111-125\">\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.\n- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.\n- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.\n\nKey insight: The visible floor has an invisible one-row cushion above it, so the objects fall under gravity but stop with one empty row before the floor or the grid bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6165ea8f\" lines=\"74-90\">\n<description>\nBuild a color-labeled shape-equivalence table from the objects listed on the right edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background contains one main 4-connected object for each foreground color.\n- The rightmost column has one singleton pixel of each foreground color, listed top-to-bottom.\n- Those singleton pixels define the row and column order of the output table.\n\nRule:\n- Normalize each main object to its tight mask and compare masks up to rotation or reflection.\n- Draw 2x2 color labels across the top and down the left, separated by one background row/column.\n- For each off-diagonal table cell, paint a 2x2 red block when the two labeled objects have the same shape under rotation/reflection, otherwise paint a 2x2 grey block.\n- Leave diagonal cells and all separators as background.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object positions and original orientations are distractors; the relation is dihedral shape equivalence, while the right-edge legend fixes the table order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8618d23e\" lines=\"9-25\">\n<description>\nStagger the two horizontal input regions onto a larger maroon separator canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a rectangular grid with an even number of rows.\n- The top half and bottom half are the two regions to preserve.\n- Maroon is absent from the input and is used only as the new separator color.\n\nRule:\n- Create an output with one extra row and one extra column, filled with maroon.\n- Copy the top half into the upper-left corner.\n- Copy the bottom half one row below the top region and one column to the right.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output is not a recoloring of either region; it inserts an L-shaped maroon\ngap by diagonally offsetting the lower half from the upper half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9344f635\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.\n- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.\n\nRule:\n- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.\n- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.\n- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.\n\nKey insight:\n- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaecdb9a\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nConvert scattered foreground objects into bottom-aligned count bars in a fixed five-color order.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and foreground objects in the task\npalette: grey, red, teal, maroon, and pink. Objects are monochromatic\n8-connected components; some palette colors may be absent.\n\nRule\nFor each palette color in order grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, count its\n8-connected objects. Create a background grid with five columns and height\nequal to the largest count, then fill each column upward from the bottom\nwith its palette color for that count.\n\nKey insight\nThe output does not preserve object shapes or positions; it is a histogram\nof per-color 8-connected object counts, using the learned palette order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"66ac4c3b\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.\n- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.\n\nRule\n- Keep the original grid unchanged.\n- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.\n- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7ec998c9\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nDraw a blue folded corner-to-corner route through the lone marker's column.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid filled with one background color.\n- Exactly one non-background marker pixel; blue is absent from the input.\n\nRule:\n- Preserve the marker and repaint background pixels in its column blue from top to bottom.\n- If the marker is in the unique center column, connect that column to the top-right and bottom-left corners.\n- Otherwise, connect the column to the top-left and bottom-right corners.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker's column is the route spine; its central vs off-center placement selects which opposite corner pair gets the horizontal edge segments.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"13e47133\" lines=\"56-69\">\n<description>\nFill partitioned regions with concentric colored bands derived from marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid partitioned into regions by wall-colored lines.\n- Each region has a dominant base color and a few isolated marker pixels of other colors at varying depths from the region boundary.\n\nRule:\n- For each region, read marker colors ordered by their Chebyshev distance from the boundary to build a color palette.\n- If no marker sits on the boundary itself, the base color leads the palette.\n- Paint every cell with palette[distance mod palette_length], producing repeating concentric rectangular frames that radiate inward.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's depth from the boundary defines its position in the cyclic palette; the pattern then tiles inward automatically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n<description>\nMove each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n  pixel of a different color.\n\nRule\n- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"38007db0\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nExtract the unique cell from each lattice row into a single-column lattice.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into equal square cells by uniform gridlines of color L.\n- Cell interiors filled with bg; each cell has an inner sprite pattern.\n- Each row of cells has exactly one cell with a unique pattern; the rest are copies.\n\nRule:\n- For each lattice row, find the cell whose inner pattern appears exactly once (the \"odd one out\").\n- Construct a single-column lattice preserving the original row heights, containing only the unique cells.\n\nKey insight: The task is per-row \"odd one out\" — every cell in a row is identical except one oddball, which must be extracted.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f1bcbc2c\" lines=\"40-55\">\n<description>\nPaint the top-connected middle channel between two orange path components teal.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is black.\n- One foreground color forms exactly two 4-connected one-pixel-wide path components.\n- Optional non-background control pixels may sit in the channel between the two paths.\n\nRule\n- Dilate each path component by one cell in the 8-neighborhood and intersect the two dilations; this gives the one-cell-wide middle channel.\n- A control pixel in that channel is a blocker only when its 4-neighbors touch fewer than both path components; otherwise it is part of the channel and is repainted.\n- After removing blockers, paint the channel component connected to the top edge teal and keep all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The desired pixels are not found by region filling; they are the geometric overlap of the two paths' one-cell halos, with weakly attached control pixels splitting the channel.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b1986d4b\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nRender the filled-square object counts as compact nested stacks ordered by count rank.\n\nInput structure\nA large noisy grid has a dominant background, scattered non-square noise, and\nfilled monochrome square objects. Each square side length has exactly one\nobject color, and side lengths form consecutive sizes.\n\nRule\nIgnore non-square components. If the lowest square in the scene is of the\nsmallest side length, treat that one smallest square as a distractor and do\nnot count it. For count rank k from left to right, create one block from all\nsquare sizes with at least k remaining objects; draw larger squares first and\nsmaller squares over their upper-left corner, then append one background\nseparator column.\n\nKey insight\nThe output is a count histogram of square side lengths, but each histogram\nslot is rendered as nested upper-left square masks using the side colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f18ec8cc\" lines=\"71-86\">\n<description>\nReorder the vertical color-major blocks while preserving every block's internal pixels.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is divided into vertical blocks, each with a unique dominant color.\nSmall marker pixels from other block colors may appear inside the blocks.\n\nRule\nIf adjacent shared-color marker rows read exactly as the current block order\nfrom right to left, reverse the blocks. Otherwise move the leftmost block to\nthe far right and shift the remaining blocks left.\n\nKey insight\nThe transformation acts on whole vertical blocks, so marker pixels move with\ntheir source block instead of being recolored or independently rearranged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
